.TH EFIBOOTMGR 8 "May 5, 2026"
.SH NAME
efibootmgr \- inspect and modify the UEFI Boot Manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.LP
.nf
\fBefibootmgr\fR [\fB-v\fR]
\fBefibootmgr\fR \fB-c\fR \fB-d\fR \fIdisk\fR \fB-p\fR \fIpartnum\fR \fB-l\fR \fIloader\fR [\fB-L\fR \fIlabel\fR]
\fBefibootmgr\fR \fB-B\fR \fB-b\fR \fINNNN\fR
\fBefibootmgr\fR \fB-o\fR \fIBootOrder\fR | \fB-O\fR
\fBefibootmgr\fR \fB-n\fR \fINNNN\fR | \fB-N\fR
\fBefibootmgr\fR \fB-t\fR \fIseconds\fR | \fB-T\fR
\fBefibootmgr\fR {\fB-a\fR | \fB-A\fR} \fB-b\fR \fINNNN\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
The \fBefibootmgr\fR utility displays and updates the boot configuration
held by the UEFI firmware in non-volatile RAM. The configuration is a
set of UEFI variables in the global namespace
(\fB8be4df61-93ca-11d2-aa0d-00e098032b8c\fR), the most important of which
are:
.RS 4
.PP
\fBBootOrder\fR \(em the sequence in which the firmware tries the boot
entries listed below.
.PP
\fBBootNNNN\fR \(em an individual boot entry, where \fINNNN\fR is a
four-hex-digit identifier; each one names a description and a UEFI
device path that points to a loader.
.PP
\fBBootCurrent\fR \(em the entry the firmware actually used on the
current boot.  Read-only from the operating system's perspective.
.PP
\fBBootNext\fR \(em an override that, when present, makes the firmware
boot the named entry once and then revert to \fBBootOrder\fR.
.PP
\fBTimeout\fR \(em how many seconds the firmware shows its menu before
booting \fBBootOrder[0]\fR.
.RE
.LP
Without arguments, \fBefibootmgr\fR prints the current state of these
variables, one per line, with each \fBBootNNNN\fR rendered on its own
line. After every successful mutation the same listing is printed
again.
.SH OPTIONS
.LP
The following options are supported. All of them require the kernel
\fBefi\fR(4D) driver and \fBPRIV_SYS_CONFIG\fR.
.TP
.BR -a " | " -A
Set or clear the \fBLOAD_OPTION_ACTIVE\fR bit on the entry selected
with \fB-b\fR. Inactive entries are skipped by the boot manager.
.TP
.BI -b " NNNN"
Boot entry to operate on, in hexadecimal. Used together with \fB-a\fR,
\fB-A\fR or \fB-B\fR.
.TP
.B -B
Delete the \fBBootNNNN\fR entry selected with \fB-b\fR and remove its
number from \fBBootOrder\fR.
.TP
.B -c
Create a new \fBBootNNNN\fR entry. The lowest unused number is
allocated, the entry is set active, and its number is prepended to
\fBBootOrder\fR. Requires \fB-d\fR, \fB-p\fR and \fB-l\fR; \fB-L\fR
sets the user-visible label (default \fBOmniOS\fR).
.TP
.BI -d " disk"
Disk to read the GPT from. Either a full \fI/dev/rdsk/\fR path or a
controller form like \fIc0t0d0\fR (translated to
\fI/dev/rdsk/c0t0d0p0\fR).
.TP
.BI -l " loader"
UEFI loader path inside the ESP, using backslashes as separators
(e.g. \fI\\\\EFI\\\\omnios\\\\loader.efi\fR).
.TP
.BI -L " label"
Human-readable label for the new entry. Default \fBOmniOS\fR.
.TP
.BI -p " partnum"
Partition number on \fIdisk\fR that the loader lives on (1-based; ESP
is usually partition 1).
.TP
.BI -n " NNNN"
Set \fBBootNext\fR to the given entry. The firmware will boot it once
and then return to \fBBootOrder\fR.
.TP
.B -N
Delete \fBBootNext\fR.
.TP
.BI -o " BootOrder"
Set \fBBootOrder\fR. \fIBootOrder\fR is a comma-separated list of
boot entry numbers in hex, e.g. \fB0001,0000,0002\fR.
.TP
.B -O
Delete \fBBootOrder\fR.
.TP
.BI -t " SECONDS"
Set \fBTimeout\fR.
.TP
.B -T
Delete \fBTimeout\fR.
.TP
.B -v
When listing, also print the EFI device path of every entry.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.LP
0 on success, 1 on a runtime error (firmware or driver), 2 on a usage
error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/efi
UEFI Runtime Services driver.
.SH SEE ALSO
.LP
.BR efivar (3LIB),
.BR uefi (5)
